00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  out into the world once more how many  people had forgotten this mask  anytime I'm given a choice between  stealth and action  I go stealth I love to hold your breath  tension of hoping that one guard over  there didn't see me and the hard-earned  triumph of executing a perfectly timed  plan designer two delivers both arming  players with stealth essentials like  hiding bodies peering through keyholes  and silent takedowns but it's also an  incredible engine for gleeful chaos one  so engrossing and amusing that I  actually accidentally beat the entire  campaign raining hilarious elaborate  death on my enemies I kick people  through skylights blasted them off  seaside cliffs lured them into  bottlenecks and watched as my carefully  placed Rapinoe grenade shredded them up  at one point I got murdered badly so I  reloaded a quick save shot a guard with  an incendiary bolt and blew up another  four with one grenade when they ran to  help  sadistic yes but also incredibly  satisfying from a gameplay standpoint  moments like that happened constantly in  Dishonored - because it's as much a toy  box as it is a game it's meant to be  experimented with it rewards and even  demands creativity this was true of the  first game and it's true here as well  mainly because the sequel simply takes  the original formula and built on it  you'll find more ways to engage enemies  without killing them like non-lethal  drop attacks and parries that start  opponents momentarily allowing you to  grab them and choke them out there are  new weapons and gadgets including  crossbow bolts that blind enemies or  send them sprinting in a chemical  induced madness weapons can be upgraded  in new ways so your starter pistol can  eventually be modded into a semi-auto  hand cannon with explosive ricocheting  rounds and most importantly there's an  entirely new protagonist with their own  set of powers  you can still play his classic hero  Corvo and enjoy all his original  supernatural abilities like pausing time  and possessing rodents but emperor's  emily kaldwin offers some exciting new  choices kiss  much like the gadgets and weaponry the  diverse and inventive mechanics inherent  in these powers turn the gameplay into a  joyful cycle of experimentation and  reward as nearly all can be used in a  variety of ways ranging from  straightforward to truly unconventional  part of what makes the experimentation  so fun is the fact that your enemies are  genuinely threatening which makes  cleverly dispatching them feel that much  more empowering they parry dodge flank  kick you away even throw rocks at you to  keep you off balance and they never  relent rather than telegraphing their  attacks or waiting patiently for you to  strike they just come at you which both  gets your adrenaline pumping and make  sure one-hit-kill counter-attacks feel  earned and if you're a stealth player  they're smart and aware enough to  present a real challenge frequently  breaking from the preset pattern  behavior observable in many other  stealth games this makes for a demanding  experience overall but weirdly the  campaign doesn't really grow more  challenging as you progress you'd think  you'd face new more intricate scenarios  or larger numbers of tougher enemies but  that's not really the case unexpected  new enemy types do emerge but feel  underutilized as they're limited to  certain levels in areas by the end I  actually felt overpowered because the  game never demanded more of me  dishonored does such a stellar job of  consistently adding new gameplay  elements it's a shame that it never  culminated in a grand all-encompassing  challenge the story also doesn't evolve  much over the course of the campaign the  original game opened with a bloody power  grab that since you want to quest for  vengeance the plot here is essentially  identical just with more cryptic occult  gibberish the plotline is fine but the  delivery proves lackluster contrary to  the gameplay the storytelling holds your  hand bombarding you with heavy-handed  exposition your character constantly  states the obvious in game then narrates  their exact thoughts and feelings over  motion comics between missions  everything I know has been turned on its  head  but I'm dealing with it the best I can  still dishonored world is undeniably  intriguing thanks mainly to the vaguely  steampunk aesthetic and the tangible  history hidden in every detail the  characters you encounter are by and  large interesting and well developed and  the expansive areas you visit feel alive  bursting with unique details there's  also plenty of side content to uncover  in the hub areas from unearthing  backstory to finding the one ultra  clever way to break into a fortified  black-market shop and of course you'll  constantly be hunting for hidden runes  some are obvious some are clever some  are just plain frustrating but you need  them to unlock new powers so get hunting  most impressively individual missions  frequently distinguish themselves by  offering a unique gameplay hook there's  a mission late in the game that involves  time manipulation and might be one of  the most unforgettable standalone  missions in a game ever it is a  masterpiece unto itself  there's also the intricate mind-bending  clockwork mansion which turns the entire  level into a giant Rubik's Cube and just  like before you can find elaborate story  driven ways to eliminate every major  target without actually killing them  dishonour to might lack challenge in its  later levels but the basic tools are a  joy to play with regardless and with two  characters and two basic play styles to  choose from both of which noticeably  impact the story and the world as you go  there's a lot of longevity to be rung  out of the campaign in fact I'm about to  start a new playthrough with no powers  on the highest difficulty setting wish  me luck  let's go

